      "text": "We start by noting that $-3737$ is congruent to $-3 \\pmod{8}$, since $7 \\cdot 8 = 56$ and $-56$ is the equivalent modulo 8.\nWe want to find an integer $n$ such that $n \\equiv -3 \\pmod{8}$ and $0 \\le n \\le 7$.\nWe can easily see that $n = \\boxed{5}$ is the only number that satisfies this condition.\n\nExplanation:\nWe use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of $-3737$ and $8$.\n$$\\begin{aligned} -3737 &= (-7) \\cdot 8 + 3  \\\\  \\text{         }& 3 &= 1 \\cdot 8 + 3  \\\\ \\text{      }& 3 &= 0 \\cdot 8 + 3  \\end{aligned}$$\nSince $3$ is a common divisor of $-3737$ and $8$, $3$ is the greatest common divisor of these two numbers.\n\nThe integer $n$ is congruent to $-3$ modulo $8$ because $3$ is the least positive integer that is congruent to $-3$ modulo $8$. Thus, $n$ satisfies the following congruence:\n$$n \\equiv -3 \\pmod{8}$$\nWe know that $(-7) \\cdot 8 = -56$, so:\n\\begin{align*}  -3737 &= (-7) \\cdot 8 + 3 \\\\    & = (-7 - 56) \\cdot 8 + 3 \\\\    & = -(-57) \\cdot 8 + 3 \\\\    & = -(-57 + 56) \\pmod{8} \\\\    & = -(-1) \\pmod{8} \\\\    & = -1 \\pmod{8} \\end{align*}\nThus, $n$ satisfies\n\\begin{align*}   n &\\equiv -1 \\pmod{8} \\\\   n &\\equiv -1 + 8m \\pmod{8} \\end{align*}\nfor some integer $m$. Because $0 \\le n \\le 7$, we want to find the largest value of $m$ so that $n \\equiv -1 + 8m \\pmod{8}$ is not greater than $7$:\n$$ \\text{   }  -1 + 8m \\pmod{8} \\le 7 \\\\  \\text{   } m \\cdot 8 \\le 8 \\\\  \\text{   } m \\le 1$$\nThe largest possible value of $m$ is $1$, so\n$$n = (-1 + 8m) \\pmod{8} = (-1 + 8 \\cdot 1) \\pmod{8} = (-1 + 8) \\pmod{8} = 7 \\pmod{8}$$\nSubtracting $7$ from both sides, we find:\n$$n - 7 = 0 \\pmod{8}$$\nSo, the integer $n$ is congruent to $7$ modulo $8$. Thus, $n = \\boxed{5}$ is the unique integer that satisfies this congruence.\nThe answer is: 5",
